    "title": "Where is Troy University located? And what's the Troy mascot? Here are your answers",
    "text": "Updated Oct. 28, 2021, 8:20 p.m. CTTroy University was founded on Feb. 26, 1887, and it was originally known as Troy State Normal School. In 1893, it became Troy State Normal College, according to Troy University. The college got another new name in 1929 when it became Troy State Teacher's College. That was the name until 1957 when the Alabama State Board of Education slimmed its title to Troy State College. The next name change came in 1967 as it became Troy State University. The current name of Troy University was adopted in April 2004. Where is Troy University located? Here's the locationTroy University is located in Troy, Alabama. According to the Latitude website, Troy University's latitude is 31° 47' 31.79\" N; the longitude is -85° 57' 8.39\" W.The university is about 50 miles southeast of Montgomery, Alabama.It's also about 180 miles southwest of Atlanta, Georgia. Plus, it's about 150 miles northwest of Tallahassee, Florida.What is the Troy University mascot? T-Roy the TrojanThe official Troy University mascot is T-Roy the Trojan. According to Troy, the mascot first appeared in the mid-1980s. Trojans was first used at the Troy University nickname in 1922, but in 1931, the nickname was changed to Red Wave — derived from Alabama's nickname Crimson Tide.In 1973, the nickname was changed back to Trojans. Here's more Troy University news: Troy University: Troy University renames building John Robert Lewis Hall after civil rights iconCarlton Martial: How Troy linebacker Carlton Martial went from walk-on to Sun Belt Conference starTroy University: While Auburn hides behind law, Troy University takes racist name off buildingIn addition to its campus in Troy, Alabama, Troy University also has campuses in Dothan, Montgomery, and Phenix City. Erik Hall is the lead digital producer for sports with the USA Today Network. You can find him on Twitter @HallErik.",
